## Solution

**Claim:** $\bigcap_i \mathfrak{p}_i = \{0\}$.

**Proof.**

First observe that every ideal contains $0$, so $\{0\} \subseteq \bigcap_i \mathfrak{p}_i$ always. We must show the reverse inclusion.

**Case 1: Some $\mathfrak{p}_j = (0)$.**

Since the $\mathfrak{p}_i$ are pairwise distinct, at most one of them can be the zero ideal (which is prime because $\mathcal{O}$ is an integral domain). If $\mathfrak{p}_j = (0)$ for some $j$, then
$$\bigcap_i \mathfrak{p}_i \subseteq \mathfrak{p}_j = (0),$$
so $\bigcap_i \mathfrak{p}_i = \{0\}$.

**Case 2: Every $\mathfrak{p}_i$ is a nonzero prime ideal.**

Recall that $\mathcal{O}$, the ring of integers of a number field, is a **Dedekind domain**. In a Dedekind domain every nonzero prime ideal is maximal, and every nonzero proper ideal admits a unique factorization into nonzero prime ideals.

Suppose, for contradiction, that there exists a nonzero $x \in \bigcap_i \mathfrak{p}_i$. Consider the principal ideal $(x)$. Since $x \neq 0$ and $x$ is not a unit (a unit cannot lie in any proper ideal, and each $\mathfrak{p}_i$ is proper), $(x)$ is a nonzero proper ideal, so it factors uniquely as
$$(x) = \mathfrak{q}_1^{e_1} \mathfrak{q}_2^{e_2} \cdots \mathfrak{q}_r^{e_r}$$
for finitely many distinct nonzero prime ideals $\mathfrak{q}_1, \dots, \mathfrak{q}_r$ and positive integers $e_1, \dots, e_r$.

A standard property of Dedekind domains is: for ideals $I, J$,
$$I \subseteq J \iff J \mid I \text{ (i.e., } I = JK \text{ for some ideal } K\text{)}.$$

Since $x \in \mathfrak{p}_i$, we have $(x) \subseteq \mathfrak{p}_i$, hence $\mathfrak{p}_i \mid (x)$. This forces $\mathfrak{p}_i \in \{\mathfrak{q}_1, \dots, \mathfrak{q}_r\}$.

But this must hold for **every** $i \in \mathbb{N}$. The set $\{\mathfrak{q}_1, \dots, \mathfrak{q}_r\}$ is finite (it has $r$ elements), while $\{\mathfrak{p}_i : i \in \mathbb{N}\}$ is an infinite set of pairwise distinct prime ideals. It is impossible to embed an infinite set of pairwise distinct elements into a finite set. **Contradiction.**

Therefore no nonzero $x$ lies in $\bigcap_i \mathfrak{p}_i$, and we conclude
$$\bigcap_i \mathfrak{p}_i = \{0\}.$$

**Remark on the existence of such a sequence.** There are indeed infinitely many nonzero prime ideals in $\mathcal{O}$: every rational prime $p$ has at least one prime ideal of $\mathcal{O}$ lying above it (since $\mathcal{O}/(p)$ is a finite ring, hence Artinian, and factors into a product of local rings corresponding to the primes above $p$), and distinct rational primes give distinct prime ideals. So a sequence $\{\mathfrak{p}_i\}_{i \in \mathbb{N}}$ of pairwise distinct nonzero prime ideals exists, and for any such sequence the intersection is $\{0\}$.

### PROOF COMPLETE
